{"pair_id": "PMC4065427_01::Lymphatic_Filariasis", "case_id": "PMC4065427_01", "target_disease": "Lymphatic_Filariasis", "decision": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "rationale": "A 56-year-old female presented with Neurofibromatosis type I and facial plexiform neurofibromas. The phrase 'elephantiasis skin' was used purely as a descriptive dermatological term for overhanging folds of neurofibroma tissue ('typically termed as elephantiasis skin'). No parasitic lymphatic filariasis was involved or suspected.", "step1_doc_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step1_document_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step2_ccu_type": "PRIMARY_CARE_EPISODE", "step3_patient_anchor": "patient_1 (56yo female)", "step4_condition_matched": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step5_assertion": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "step5_concept_grounding": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step6_temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "step7_clinical_role": "NON_DIAGNOSTIC_CONTEXT", "step8_case_definition": "NOT_MET", "step9_evidence_strength": "E4_CONTEXT", "step9_key_evidence": {"evidence_items": [{"source_field": "case_text", "start_char": 640, "end_char": 790, "source_quote": "On physical examination, she was pale and face was disfigured with growth of completely overhanging skin folds typically termed as elephantiasis skin.", "patient_anchor_id": "patient_1", "test_name": "Dermatological physical examination", "target_concept": "Elephantiasis skin (descriptive)", "specimen": null, "result": "DESCRIPTIVE_FINDING", "temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "relation": "REFUTES", "strength": "E4_CONTEXT"}]}, "step10_contradiction": "NON_TARGET_HOMONYM", "step10_contradiction_flag": true, "step11_vpd_satisfied": false, "step12_dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "step12_audit": {"annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-11-04T04:42:28.238853+00:00", "confidence": "HIGH"}, "annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "confidence": "HIGH"}
{"pair_id": "PMC4591341_01::Lymphatic_Filariasis", "case_id": "PMC4591341_01", "target_disease": "Lymphatic_Filariasis", "decision": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "rationale": "A 54-year-old male presented with facial swellings due to rhinoentomophthoromycosis (Conidiobolus coronatus). Although venous blood examination detected microfilariae, they were explicitly identified as Loa loa (loiasis), an entirely distinct non-target subcutaneous filarial parasite, not lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria/Brugia). Facial elephantiasis was a clinical descriptor for the fungal edema. There is no claim for lymphatic filariasis.", "step1_doc_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step1_document_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step2_ccu_type": "PRIMARY_CARE_EPISODE", "step3_patient_anchor": "patient_1 (54yo male)", "step4_condition_matched": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step5_assertion": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "step5_concept_grounding": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step6_temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "step7_clinical_role": "NON_DIAGNOSTIC_CONTEXT", "step8_case_definition": "NOT_MET", "step9_evidence_strength": "E0_CONTRADICTED", "step9_key_evidence": {"evidence_items": [{"source_field": "case_text", "start_char": 932, "end_char": 1111, "source_quote": "Microfilaria of Loa Loa were detected in venous EDTA blood (Citrate-Saponin acid method) and stool samples revealed an infection with Ascaris lumbricoides and Trichuris trichiura.", "patient_anchor_id": "patient_1", "test_name": "Venous blood microfilaria examination", "target_concept": "Loa loa microfilariae (non-target filaria)", "specimen": "venous EDTA blood", "result": "POSITIVE_NON_TARGET", "temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "relation": "REFUTES", "strength": "E0_CONTRADICTED"}]}, "step10_contradiction": "NON_TARGET_PARASITE", "step10_contradiction_flag": true, "step11_vpd_satisfied": false, "step12_dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "step12_audit": {"annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-11-04T04:42:28.238885+00:00", "confidence": "HIGH"}, "annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "confidence": "HIGH"}
{"pair_id": "PMC3236059_01::Lymphatic_Filariasis", "case_id": "PMC3236059_01", "target_disease": "Lymphatic_Filariasis", "decision": "EXCLUDED_NON_CASE_REPORT", "dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_CASE_REPORT", "rationale": "This publication is an educational quiz consisting of a 4-sentence vignette about scrotal swelling following epididymitis accompanied by general study questions ('What are the common causes of scrotal elephantiasis?'). It lacks a complete clinical case workup, investigation, or diagnosis of filariasis.", "step1_doc_type": "EXCLUDED_NON_CASE_REPORT", "step1_document_type": "EXCLUDED_NON_CASE_REPORT", "step2_ccu_type": "NON_CLINICAL_DOCUMENT", "step3_patient_anchor": "patient_1 (39yo male, hypothetical vignette)", "step4_condition_matched": "Lymphatic_Filariasis", "step5_assertion": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "step5_concept_grounding": "TARGET_CONFIRMED", "step6_temporality": "UNKNOWN", "step7_clinical_role": "EPIDEMIOLOGICAL_CONTEXT", "step8_case_definition": "NOT_MET", "step9_evidence_strength": "E4_CONTEXT", "step9_key_evidence": {"evidence_items": [{"source_field": "case_text", "start_char": 0, "end_char": 122, "source_quote": "A 39-year-old man presents with a several-year history of an enlarged scrotum that developed after a bout of epididymitis.", "patient_anchor_id": "patient_1", "test_name": "Educational case presentation", "target_concept": "Post-epididymitis scrotal lymphedema", "specimen": null, "result": "CLINICAL_VIGNETTE", "temporality": "UNKNOWN", "relation": "REFUTES", "strength": "E4_CONTEXT"}]}, "step10_contradiction": "EDUCATIONAL_QUIZ", "step10_contradiction_flag": true, "step11_vpd_satisfied": false, "step12_dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_CASE_REPORT", "step12_audit": {"annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-11-04T04:42:28.238893+00:00", "confidence": "HIGH"}, "annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "confidence": "HIGH"}
{"pair_id": "PMC6920227_01::Lymphatic_Filariasis", "case_id": "PMC6920227_01", "target_disease": "Lymphatic_Filariasis", "decision": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "rationale": "A 67-year-old male presented with penoscrotal swelling diagnosed as Idiopathic Peno-Scrotal Elephantiasis Nostras Verrucosa (ENV). The authors explicitly define ENV as chronic non-filarial lymphedema caused by bacterial or non-infectious obstruction. No filarial infection was identified.", "step1_doc_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step1_document_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step2_ccu_type": "PRIMARY_CARE_EPISODE", "step3_patient_anchor": "patient_1 (67yo male)", "step4_condition_matched": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step5_assertion": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "step5_concept_grounding": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step6_temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "step7_clinical_role": "NON_DIAGNOSTIC_CONTEXT", "step8_case_definition": "NOT_MET", "step9_evidence_strength": "E4_CONTEXT", "step9_key_evidence": {"evidence_items": [{"source_field": "case_text", "start_char": 2819, "end_char": 2950, "source_quote": "The patient was diagnosed with Idiopathic Peno-Scrotal Elephantiasis Nostras Verrucosa (ENV) based on Clinicopathological criteria.", "patient_anchor_id": "patient_1", "test_name": "Histopathological and clinicopathological evaluation", "target_concept": "Elephantiasis Nostras Verrucosa (non-filarial)", "specimen": "skin biopsy", "result": "POSITIVE", "temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "relation": "REFUTES", "strength": "E4_CONTEXT"}]}, "step10_contradiction": "NON_TARGET_HOMONYM", "step10_contradiction_flag": true, "step11_vpd_satisfied": false, "step12_dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "step12_audit": {"annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-11-04T04:42:28.238902+00:00", "confidence": "HIGH"}, "annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "confidence": "HIGH"}
{"pair_id": "PMC6207953_01::Lymphatic_Filariasis", "case_id": "PMC6207953_01", "target_disease": "Lymphatic_Filariasis", "decision": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "rationale": "A 59-year-old male with chronic cardiovascular and peripheral vascular disease developed lower leg angiosarcoma (Stewart-Treves syndrome) on long-standing stasis lymphedema. The term elephantiasis was used to describe chronic non-filarial skin changes ('chronic changes of elephantiasis, specifically verrucous skin'). Lymphatic filariasis was not involved.", "step1_doc_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step1_document_type": "CLINICAL_CASE_REPORT", "step2_ccu_type": "PRIMARY_CARE_EPISODE", "step3_patient_anchor": "patient_1 (59yo male)", "step4_condition_matched": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step5_assertion": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "step5_concept_grounding": "NON_TARGET_CONCEPT", "step6_temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "step7_clinical_role": "NON_DIAGNOSTIC_CONTEXT", "step8_case_definition": "NOT_MET", "step9_evidence_strength": "E4_CONTEXT", "step9_key_evidence": {"evidence_items": [{"source_field": "case_text", "start_char": 395, "end_char": 591, "source_quote": "Physical examination revealed chronic changes of elephantiasis, specifically verrucous skin, with a noticeable serosanguinous foul-smelling discharge, more prominent on the right than the left leg", "patient_anchor_id": "patient_1", "test_name": "Clinical physical examination", "target_concept": "Non-filarial elephantiasis", "specimen": null, "result": "DESCRIPTIVE_FINDING", "temporality": "CURRENT_EPISODE", "relation": "REFUTES", "strength": "E4_CONTEXT"}]}, "step10_contradiction": "NON_TARGET_HOMONYM", "step10_contradiction_flag": true, "step11_vpd_satisfied": false, "step12_dataset_label": "EXCLUDED_NON_TARGET_CASE", "step12_audit": {"annotator_id": "disease-agent-lymphatic_filariasis", "annotation_version": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-11-04T04:42:28.238937+00:00", "confidence": "HIGH"}, "annotator_id"